# Product Blueprint — portable, single file

> **If you are an agent working in the repo this file was authored in, this is not the file to
> read.** Read `.claude/skills/product-blueprint/SKILL.md` plus that repo's map instead — this
> file deliberately omits every repo-specific fact, so following it here loses the token names,
> the landmines and the house literals. This file exists only to be copied into a *different*
> product.

Everything needed to build an **agent console** — a product where a small operations team
configures autonomous agents, watches what they did, and works the records that come out — with
**no reference to any specific repo, design system or library**. Drop this one file into a new
project and the whole system travels with it.

> **This is a snapshot, deliberately forked.** It duplicates the multi-file skill it came from.
> The moment you copy it into a new product, **this copy is canonical there** and the original is
> canonical where it lives. Don't try to keep them in sync — they describe different products.

**Install into a new project** (three steps, in order):

1. Save this file as `.claude/skills/product-blueprint/SKILL.md` in the new repo, and give it the
   YAML frontmatter in Appendix A. Parts 0–6 below are the skill.
2. Split Part 7 into four files under `.claude/agents/` — one per agent, frontmatter included.
   Each already refers to "this skill", so nothing needs rewiring.
3. **Write your repo map before the second feature** using the template in Part 8. Until it
   exists, every agent derives conventions by reading your code — which works, but slowly, and
   it re-derives them every session.

---

## Part 0 — The one rule, if you only keep one

**Copy the nearest working precedent. A new module that looks different is a new convention** —
and a second way to do the same thing costs more than the feature earned. Before writing
anything: find the closest existing surface, read it *in full* (the comments carry the
constraints), and match its structure, spacing rhythm and comment density. Name the file you
copied from when you report.

**If the product is new and there is no precedent**, that rule has nothing to bite on — so the
first move is instead to **classify the surface** (Part 1). The classification fixes the shell,
the state model and which states you owe the user; everything else follows from it.

Corollary, and the one that saves the most wasted work: **the code outranks every doc, including
this one.** Where a written rule and the dominant pattern in the codebase disagree, the doc is
stale. Before flagging a violation, grep how often the "violation" appears — if it is the
dominant pattern, it is the convention.

