## Part 3 — The seam convention

This is what makes UI-ahead-of-backend safe. Design finishes before the service exists; the seam
is how you ship that without lying.

**7. Tag the boundary `[SEAM]`** in a doc comment on every function touching an endpoint that
isn't live, saying what happens on failure.

**8. Write the endpoint table now**, in the module's data or catalog file — the routes the
handlers will map onto when the service lands, and the shape each returns. This is the handoff to
whoever builds the API and it is worth more than a ticket.

**The data file's table is canonical**; the `[SEAM]` comment names its own route in one line and
nothing more. Two copies of a spec drift and the reader can't tell which is current.

**The api-util ships complete and uncalled.** Write the real functions, then don't wire them up.
Exports don't trip unused-symbol checks, so the module typechecks; the page makes no request and
renders the real empty state, so **there is no loading or error state to write yet** — a
`loading` flag nothing reads is an unused local and fails a strict build. When the service lands,
the page grows the effect and both states together, and the api-util needs no change.

**9. Never fabricate data at a seam.** Show the **real** empty state and keep any action needing
the absent service **disabled**. Three boundaries decide the ambiguous cases:

- **Session-local state is fine.** A list in `useState` that resets on reload is honest — the
  change really happened, it just isn't persisted. Say so in the comment.
- **A success toast is honest iff the change it announces actually occurred**, including in
  session-only state. It is dishonest when it claims a *remote* effect that did not happen — a
  delivery sent, a test fired, a record persisted that a reload will lose.
- **A labelled example is not fabrication.** A payload preview or schema sample shown *as* an
  example, the way API docs do, is legitimate — fixed value, comment saying it's an illustration.
  What's forbidden is invented data presented as *the tenant's own*: fake rows in their list,
  fake history, fake metrics.

A demo with fake rows gets approved, and the gap surfaces in production.

**10. Gate an unfinished integration behind an exported `const` flag**, with a comment saying
what to flip and what else must change with it. Keep the machinery, disable the entry point.

