## Part 4 — UI system

### Why plain divs and inline styles

If your design system ships as custom elements (Stencil, Lit, any web-component build), it
renders into **shadow DOM**. Three consequences drive nearly every rule here:

1. **Layout doesn't propagate.** `flex: 1`, `height: 100%` and `min-height: 0` stop at a shadow
   host. A full-height flex column built from design-system containers collapses, and no CSS on
   the host fixes it.
2. **CSS custom properties *do* cross the boundary.** This is the escape hatch: to override
   something baked into a component's internals, redefine the variable it reads, scoped to that
   one host — `style={{ ['--radius-token' as string]: 'var(--radius-round)' }}`.
3. **Slotted children behave differently from props.** A prop the component doesn't fully
   implement fails silently; a child flowing into the default slot is laid out by the component's
   internal flex — often the working alternative when a prop is broken.

**Token names in the examples below are illustrative.** Substitute your own design system's — and
record the real ones in your repo map (Part 8), because that is the fact people get wrong most.

**11. Token-only values.** No hex, no raw px for spacing, radius or colour. If the semantic token
you want doesn't exist, **reuse the nearest one** rather than inventing a value — an invented
value is invisible to theming and survives forever. Let tokens handle light/dark; never branch on
theme in component code.

**Verify a token exists in the design system's compiled stylesheet, not in a hand-written token
doc.** The two drift, and an *undefined* token is worse than a wrong one: it silently resolves to
nothing, so a gap becomes 0 and a `font:` shorthand falls back to inherited type — which reads as
"the token is mis-sized" and sends you to patch the symptom. Grep the stylesheet, list what
actually exists, and record the real names in your repo map (Part 8).

One exception, and it must be *shared* rather than local: where the scale genuinely has no step
for a one-off structural inset (a large empty-state pad, a viewport-relative cap), the codebase
settles on a literal and repeats it verbatim. Copy the existing literal exactly; never re-derive
a nearby value. If you're the second use, hoist it to a named constant.

**A new semantic colour belongs in the app's own token layer**, defined for both themes, not
inline at a call site.

**12. Plain `<div>` for layout. Inline `style={{}}` for everything.** Use the design system for
*controls* (buttons, inputs, selects, icons) and plain divs for *structure*. Stylesheets stay for
genuine keyframes and third-party overrides only.

**The card and row ring is an inset box-shadow, not a `border`:**

```ts
boxShadow: 'inset 0 0 0 1px var(--colors-border-neutral-base-subtle)'
```

The ring draws **inside** the box. Three things follow, and together they make this the house
card language rather than a preference: a ringed row can't push past its container (a border adds
2px and overflows a fixed-width column); nothing shifts when the ring appears on hover or
selection; and it composes with elevation on one property. Reach for `border: 1px solid` and new
surfaces are subtly wrong everywhere — hard to see in isolation, obvious beside an existing card.
Dividers inside a surface follow the same logic: a 1px background-coloured div, not a border edge.

**These rules govern code you write, not components you reuse.** Older shared components built
from design-system containers predate the convention and are **still the right thing to
import** — don't rewrite them, and don't treat their internals as licence to build new surfaces
that way.

**13. Text is a plain `<span>`**, not the design system's text component (its shadow host brings
shorthand and line-clamp quirks). Two ways to style it, and the choice is mechanical:

```tsx
// Default — the `font:` shorthand token: family, size, weight, line-height in one property.
<span style={{ font: 'var(--text-body-reg-14)', color: 'var(--colors-text-main)' }}>{title}</span>
```

```ts
// When you must override size or line-height, or for a shared row primitive many callers spread:
export const bodyRegStyle: React.CSSProperties = {
  fontFamily: '<your stack>', fontSize: '14px', fontWeight: 400, lineHeight: '20px',
}
```

- **Never both in one style object.** A `font:` shorthand beside a `fontSize` warns on every
  render. Easy to hit, because the shorthand arrives from a token and the override is added later
  by someone who can't see it. Needing an override is the signal to switch that element to
  longhand **entirely** — don't patch the shorthand.
- **The shorthand bakes in line-height**, so it can't be overridden alongside. Move to longhand,
  or redefine the whole token for that scope.
- **Colour at the call site**, both ways. The scale is size and weight; colour is semantic.

**14. Page shell = fixed header + exactly one scroll region.**

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ header: title / subtitle / action  FIXED│  outside the scroller
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ below-header slot (tabs)           FIXED│  optional, outside the scroller
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ ░░ body — the ONLY scroll region ░░░░░░ │  centred max-width column
└─────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

```tsx
<div style={{ height: '100%', display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column',
              overflow: 'hidden', boxSizing: 'border-box' }}>
  <div style={{ flexShrink: 0, padding: HEADER_PAD }}>
    <div style={{ maxWidth, width: '100%', margin: '0 auto' }}>{header}</div>
  </div>

  <Scroller style={{ flex: 1, minHeight: 0 }}>
    <div style={{ padding: BODY_PAD,
                  ...(fillBody ? { height: '100%', display: 'flex',
                                   flexDirection: 'column' } : null) }}>
      <div style={{ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', gap: SECTION_GAP,
                    maxWidth, width: '100%', margin: '0 auto',
                    ...(fillBody ? { flex: 1, minHeight: 0 } : null) }}>
        {children}
      </div>
    </div>
  </Scroller>
</div>
```

- `minHeight: 0` on the scroller is what lets a flex child actually shrink; without it the body
  grows and the whole page scrolls instead.
- The **same `maxWidth` and inset** on header and body is why nothing jumps as you scroll.
- **`fillBody` must be static per page, never derived from content.** Toggle it when the first
  row arrives and you remount the height constraint underneath a table's measuring effect, and
  the table mis-sizes. A page with a table is always filled, even when empty.

**15. Section → header → body.** Collapse the header-to-body gap to zero when a section has no
children, or empty sections reserve visible whitespace (`React.Children.toArray` filters
`null`/`false`, so it detects "renders nothing" correctly). If sections animate their height:
measure the inner node with `ResizeObserver` and transition the outer height — and **guard nested
animations**, because a child running its own height transition fires the observer at frame rate.
Time-detect it: if a fire lands within ~80ms of the last, track the height with **no** transition
and `overflow: visible`, so your lag doesn't clip the child.

**Dividers separate sections and nothing else** — never between rows inside one section.
Whitespace groups rows; a divider inside a group reads as a section break and fragments the page.

**16. A setting is a row:** text stack left, control right. On a narrow container the control
**wraps beneath** the text rather than crushing it.

```tsx
<div style={{ display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', justifyContent: 'space-between',
              flexWrap: 'wrap', gap: 'var(--gap-row) var(--gap-col)', width: '100%' }}>
  <div style={{ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', gap: TIGHT,
                flex: '1 1 240px', minWidth: 0 }}>
    <span style={{ ...bodyRegStyle, color: TEXT_MAIN }}>{title}</span>
    {subtitle && <span style={{ ...captionRegStyle, color: TEXT_CAPTION }}>{subtitle}</span>}
  </div>
  <div style={{ flexShrink: 0 }}>{control}</div>
</div>
```

`flex: 1 1 240px` + `flexWrap` is the whole trick; `minWidth: 0` lets the text truncate rather
than force the row wider.

**17. The record triad** — list page, create/edit dialog, detail view.

```tsx
const [items, setItems] = useState<Item[]>([])
const [dialogOpen, setDialogOpen] = useState(false)
const [editing, setEditing] = useState<Item | null>(null)   // null = create

// Visibility split from the record so the body renders through the close animation.
const [detail, setDetail] = useState<Item | null>(null)
const [detailOpen, setDetailOpen] = useState(false)

const [pendingDelete, setPendingDelete] = useState<Item | null>(null)
```

Four state shapes, four jobs; `editing: null` doubling as "create mode" keeps one dialog serving
both. Keep an open detail view in step with an edit:
`setDetail(prev => prev && prev.id === id ? { ...prev, ...values } : prev)`.

Five details that are always wrong the first time:

- **Split modal visibility from the record** *when the body is a form or an expensive tree*, or
  the body unmounts the instant you clear the record and the close animation plays on an empty
  box. For a confirmation whose copy is **static**, deriving visibility from the record
  (`visible={pending !== null}`) is fine — the cost is a brief cosmetic blank. **But if the copy
  interpolates the record, snapshot it or split visibility**, or a destructive confirmation
  spends its exit reading "We'll stop sending events to **.**" — losing the identity of the thing
  it names at the moment the user decides.
- **A clickable row's action menu must swallow its clicks** —
  `<span onClick={e => e.stopPropagation()}>`. Needed even for menu items rendered through a
  portal: React events bubble through the component tree, not the DOM tree.
- **A whole-row toggle wrapping an interactive control needs a keyboard guard.** If the row is
  `role="button" tabIndex={0}` with an `onKeyDown` and contains a focusable checkbox, Space fires
  the toggle *twice* — once via `onChange`, once as the keydown bubbles. With functional updaters
  the two compose to a no-op and the control looks dead. Stopping the *click* does not cover the
  keyboard path: guard with `if (e.target !== e.currentTarget) return`.
- **A disabled primary action must say why.** Render the missing requirement beside it, in the
  order the form asks for things. A dead button with no explanation is the main cost of
  disabled-until-valid, and it is cheap to avoid.
- **A create that returns a one-time credential needs a second view**, not a toast. Morph the
  modal from form to credential and disable escape-to-close while it shows — an unrecoverable
  value must not be dismissible by reflex.

**18. Overlay decision rule.**

| Use | When | Note |
|---|---|---|
| **Modal** | A focused task owns the screen | Create/edit forms, multi-step |
| **Side sheet** | Context alongside what's behind | Detail you compare against the list |
| **Popover** | Anchored menu or small detail | Re-measure after async content loads |
| **Tooltip** | A label, nothing more | Never put actions in one |
| **Confirmation** | Destructive action | Name the consequence, not "are you sure?" |

**A tall modal scrolls its own body.** Hand scrolling to a scroller inside the body and set
`overflow: hidden` on the modal's content wrapper so the two don't nest. Clamp with
`maxHeight: 'calc(100vh - <chrome>px)'` and **hoist that literal to one shared constant** — both
halves of a create/detail pair need the identical value and two copies drift. Reset scroll when
the content identity changes (a `scrollResetKey`), or the second record opens halfway down.

Two overlay bugs worth pre-empting: **async content breaks positioning** (a popover measured
before its icons load hangs off the viewport edge — re-measure with a `ResizeObserver`), and
**tooltips stick** (focus restored to a trigger re-shows its tooltip; a missed `mouseleave`
leaves it up — show on keyboard focus only, plus a `pointermove` watchdog). A tooltip on a
*disabled* control needs its own wrapper to receive pointer events; that is how a disabled action
explains itself.

**19. Write the three states before the happy path** — *once the surface fetches*.

- **Empty** — illustration, a title, one line on what will appear here and why, and the primary
  action. State what the surface *will* hold, not that it's blank.
- **Loading** — a skeleton shaped like the final content, not a centred spinner, so the layout
  doesn't jump when data lands.
- **Error** — the backend's message verbatim, with a retry.

Most surfaces spend most of their early life empty, and a list built happy-path-first gets an
empty state bolted on badly. At a seam that makes **no request**, only the empty state applies —
absent loading/error states are correct there, not an omission.

**Icons, if they load from a CDN by name:** they resolve asynchronously and a wrong name fails
silently — the glyph is simply absent. Probe a new name before using it (`curl` the URL) and
**check the real success condition** — a CDN answering a miss with `403` rather than `404` will
make a `404`-testing script conclude every missing icon exists. Try a different group when a name
misses. Set the non-interactive flag on decorative icons so they don't carry click-target
padding. And distinguish the animated spinner glyph from a static "in progress" one — they have
different names, and the animated one spins forever in a badge.

### Motion

Motion is part of the design system, not decoration added at the end. It also fails differently
from layout: a wrong duration is invisible in a screenshot, in a typecheck and in a code review,
and only shows up as "this feels cheap". Nine rules cover nearly all of it.

**20. Two easings do almost everything, and they are not interchangeable.** A standard curve for
*state changes* — colour, hover, focus, small position shifts: `cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)`. A
hard-decelerating curve for *entrances and size changes*: `cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`
(out-quart) or `cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)` (out-expo). Overshoot
(`cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1)`) is for a deliberate pop and nothing else — on a panel it
reads as a bug. **Hoist them as named constants.** An easing copied inline across dozens of call
sites can never be retuned, which is the same failure as an untokenised colour.

**21. Duration bands, not arbitrary numbers.**

| Band | For | Note |
|---|---|---|
| ~150ms | hover, colour, focus ring | below ~100ms reads as instant; above ~200ms feels laggy under a pointer |
| ~280–320ms | panels, section height, modal open, smart resize | the structural band — most of the product lives here |
| ~360ms | overlay exit | the longest thing a user waits through before the UI is idle |
| 1.2–2.4s, looping | shimmer, pulse, breathing dots | **ambient only** — never a blocking transition |

**22. Open and close are not mirror images.** Open decelerates hard and lands (out-expo); close
wants a symmetric s-curve (ease-in-out cubic). **Reusing the open curve on the close is the
default mistake** — an in-expo close keeps the element nearly stationary for ~70% of its duration
and then snaps, which doesn't read as smooth.

The *duration* asymmetry has no universal direction — it follows the gesture. Something that
**scales in place** wants a close slightly *longer* than its open, because the shrink has to stay
visible the whole way. Something that **translates off-screen** wants a close *shorter* than its
open, because once it's leaving the viewport there is nothing left to watch. Pick per overlay
type, and keep both profiles in named constants so the difference is legible.

**23. Layers that leave together must finish together.** A backdrop fade and its card share one
duration, or a single frame shows an opaque card sitting on an already-cleared backdrop. And
unmount on the **slower** of the two — otherwise the fade is yanked mid-dissolve. Scale gently in,
further out (≈0.94 → 1 on enter, → ≈0.85 on exit): the exit can be a pure scale-down because the
backdrop fade carries the dissolve, so it needs more travel to read.

**24. The exit animation is where data bugs surface.** Whatever the exit renders must still have
its data for the *whole* duration. This is the mechanism behind rule 17's visibility split: for
~360ms a confirmation with interpolated copy reads "We'll stop sending events to **.**" — losing
the name of the thing it names, exactly as the user decides. Any animation that outlives the state
it renders has this bug; the modal is just where you meet it first.

**25. Reduced motion is a hard requirement, honoured through one shared helper.** Every animated
component checks `matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)')` and skips to the end state
rather than shortening the duration. **Write the helper once and import it everywhere.** It is the
single most-copied function in a codebase like this, and once a dozen local copies exist the
policy can no longer be changed at all — you cannot even audit who honours it.

**26. Animate a *measured* height, never `height: auto`.** `auto` doesn't interpolate. Measure the
content with `ResizeObserver` and transition the container — plus the nested-animation guard in
rule 15, without which a child's own height transition drives the observer at frame rate.

**27. Streaming and appended content animate opacity only.** For text that arrives token by token,
wrap **only the growing tail** and animate opacity — no blur, no transform. Blur on every
animating word forces a per-frame repaint of the whole block and a long reply will hang the page;
transforms break inline wrapping. Keys must be stable so already-shown words never re-animate, and
the whole effect must be scoped to the streaming state — reopening a saved thread renders its
answer instantly.

**28. A looping glyph is not a progress indicator.** A spinner that spins forever in a badge tells
the user nothing and implies the system is stuck. Ambient loops signal *liveness*; state changes
signal *progress*. Don't substitute one for the other.

For a whole-document swap (theme, locale) reach for the platform's view-transition API where it
exists, with a plain synchronous fallback for unsupported browsers **and** for reduced motion —
that fallback is not optional, it's the same rule 25 path.

