## Part 8 — Your repo map template

Save as `.claude/skills/product-blueprint/references/<your-repo>-map.md`. **This is the only file
that may contain paths, library names or token names.** Everything above stays portable; every
concrete fact goes here. Write it as you learn each fact — a map written from memory months later
is the one that goes stale silently.

```markdown
# <Repo> map

Where every rule in the blueprint actually lives here, plus the landmines.
**Read this whenever the work is inside `<repo>/`.** Paths are relative to `<app package>/`.

## ⚠ Superseded docs
Any committed doc that the newest code deliberately contradicts, quoted, with the file that
overrides it and why. Reviewing against a stale doc is the top source of false findings.

## Where tokens actually come from
The **compiled stylesheet** that defines them (usually inside a design-system package under
`node_modules`), how it reaches the app (which import pulls it in), and the app's own
override/extension layer. Include a grep command so the list is self-verifying, plus the real
token names that exist — especially any the hand-written docs get wrong.

## Pattern → implementation
A table: rule → the file that implements it. Then name the three-to-five files to **imitate**
when building something new. Locate by symbol or comment banner, not line number — line numbers
in an actively developed repo rot within weeks.

## Product surfaces
What exists today, by nav section, plus anything mounted outside the nav. This is what stops a
new design proposing a second way to do something the product already does.

## Design-system landmines
Every prop that doesn't work, every token whose name lies, every component whose internals
resist. **Each one earned the hard way — write it down the day you lose the afternoon.** Include
the shape of the failure, not just the fix, so the next person recognises the symptom.

## Do not flag these — settled conventions
The false positives a reviewer produces here, each with why the code is right. Keep this list
closed: re-raising a settled convention is how a review loses credibility.

## Established house values — copy verbatim
Literals with no token step, their exact string, and their call-site count. Copy the literal;
never re-derive a nearby value.

## House conventions not visible in the code
The rules nothing enforces: error-copy policy, forbidden words, the fabrication ban, how UI
gets verified.

## Commands
The typecheck command (THE feedback loop), dev, build — and any script that is **broken**, with
why, so nobody reads its failure as a code problem. Path aliases.
```

